Pod

Introduction: 

Explain what problem Kubernetes solves.

Talking Points

	Traditional deployment: application running on servers

	Containerization with Docker

	Need for orchestration when containers scale

	Kubernetes manages containers automatically

Key Concept

	👉 The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes is a Pod.


	“In Kubernetes we don’t deploy containers directly. We deploy Pods, and Pods contain one or more containers.”


================================================================================

What is a Pod?

Definition

	A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes that can run one or more containers.

	Pod Characteristics

	Each Pod has its unique IP Address within the cluster.

	Contains one or more containers

	Containers share:

		Network

		Storage

		IP Address

		Runs on a Node

================================================================================

Architecture:

Kubernetes Cluster
       │
       ├── Node 1
       │      └── Pod
       │           ├── Container A
       │           └── Container B
       │
       └── Node 2
              └── Pod
                   └── Container

================================================================================
Pod Lifecycle

Explain states.

Pod Phases

	1️⃣ Pending
	Pod accepted but containers not started.

	2️⃣ Running
	Containers are running.

	3️⃣ Succeeded
	Containers finished successfully.

	4️⃣ Failed
	Container exited with error.

	5️⃣ Unknown
	Kubernetes cannot determine state.

