Pod Limitations

	Pods are not meant to be created manually in production.

Problems:

	Pods can die  

	Pods are not auto recreated

Instead we use:

	Deployment

	ReplicaSet

	StatefulSet

	DaemonSet


================================================================================

Best Practices

	Use Deployments instead of Pods
	✔ Use resource limits
	✔ Use health checks
	✔ Avoid multi-container pods unless needed

Type Of Pods:


One-container-per-pod or Single Container Pod. 

	Single Container Pod (Most Common)

	This is the most commonly used Pod type.

	👉 One Pod contains only one container.

	Diagram
	+----------------------+
	|        POD           |
	|                      |
	|   +---------------+  |
	|   |   Container   |  |
	|   |    (App)      |  |
	|   |   nginx       |  |
	|   +---------------+  |
	|                      |
	+----------------------+

================================================================================


Multi-container-pod or Sidecar  containers  

	In this case, a Pod runs multiple containers together.

		Containers share:

			Network

			Storage

			Same IP

			Lifecycle

		Real-World Architecture
		+--------------------------------+
		|              POD               |
		|                                |
		|  Django API Container          |
		|                                |
		|  +--------------------------+  |
		|  | Django App               |  |
		|  +--------------------------+  |
		|                                |
		|  Sidecar Container             |
		|                                |
		|  +--------------------------+  |
		|  | Log Forwarder (filebeat)  | |
		|  +--------------------------+  |
		|                                |
		+--------------------------------+


